,"url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"`UpstreamError`","locale":"en"},"11039":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-134","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"`upstream_error`","locale":"en"},"11040":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-135","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"502","locale":"en"},"11041":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-136","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"5","locale":"en"},"11042":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-137","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"`ModelUnavailableError`","locale":"en"},"11043":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-138","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"`model_unavailable`","locale":"en"},"11044":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-139","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"503","locale":"en"},"11045":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-140","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"5","locale":"en"},"11046":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-141","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"A provider's `ProviderError` (see Provider adapters) is a subclass of `UpstreamError` that carries the upstream status: when the upstream answered with a 4xx or 5xx, that status goes to the client unchanged.","locale":"en"},"11047":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-142","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"`hoshimi/api/errors.py` picks the error's shape by path:","locale":"en"},"11048":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-143","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"`/v1/messages` and `/v1/messages/count_tokens`: Anthropic's `{\"type\": \"error\", \"error\": {\"type\", \"message\"}}`; an upstream error on the native pass-through returns the upstream's own body and headers.","locale":"en"},"11049":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-144","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"Any other `/v1/` path: OpenAI's `{\"error\": {\"message\", \"type\", \"code\"}}`.","locale":"en"},"11050":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-145","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"The management API: `{\"code\", \"message\", \"detail\"}`.","locale":"en"},"11051":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-146","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#errors","content":"The command line's `main()` catches `HoshimiError`, logs its message and exits with its exit code; Ctrl+C exits with 130, and any other unexpected exception prints a traceback and exits with 1.","locale":"en"},"11052":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-147","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#one-request-end-to-end","content":"Taking `POST /v1/chat/completions`:","locale":"en"},"11053":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-148","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#middleware-and-route","content":"`SecurityMiddleware` checks the `Host`, the origin, the client address and the model API key first. The route function is thin: it parses the body, calls into `core` and turns the result into a response.","locale":"en"},"11054":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-149","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#preparing-the-request","content":"`prepare_request` in `hoshimi/api/inference.py` fills in the default model when the request has no `model` (`server.default_model`, or the pool's default), and refuses with `ValidationError` when there is none; it also gives the request a `prompt_cache_key` (one the request brings is kept).","locale":"en"},"11055":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-150","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#counting-it","content":"`tracked_chunks` opens the usage record (`stats.turn`) before any provider is asked, so even a request that fails to route is counted. The reasoning effort is recorded as the level the request actually carries; the account that answered is reported by the pool when the first chunk arrives (`on_route`).","locale":"en"},"11056":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-151","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#routing-to-a-provider","content":"`Registry.resolve` turns the model name into a provider and an upstream model name:","locale":"en"},"11057":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-152","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#routing-to-a-provider","content":"`provider/model` whose prefix is an enabled provider: that provider.","locale":"en"},"11058":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-153","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#routing-to-a-provider","content":"A bare model name: the first provider, in provider order (`PROVIDER_ORDER`), whose catalogue has it.","locale":"en"},"11059":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-154","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#routing-to-a-provider","content":"No catalogue has it but only one provider is enabled: passed through to that provider.","locale":"en"},"11060":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-155","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#routing-to-a-provider","content":"Otherwise `model_not_found`.","locale":"en"},"11061":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-156","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#routing-to-a-provider","content":"`Registry.stream` then applies the reasoning-effort policy (turning the level into the model's own wire spelling) and checks the dead-model record: once every account answered 404/410 for a model, it is noted for a while, and requests for it are refused at once with `model_unavailable` (503) instead of asking the upstream again.","locale":"en"},"11062":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-157","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#account-rotation-and-the-upstream-protocol","content":"`Provider.stream_using` tries the provider's accounts in turn: a failed credential is renewed and then rotated, an account out of allowance cools down for a while, and a saturated model stops the round at once. The chosen account sends the request in the upstream's own protocol through `stream_raw`, which turns the upstream stream into **normalized chunks**. The details are in Provider adapters.","locale":"en"},"11063":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-158","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"`emit_chat_chunks` turns normalized chunks into `chat.completion.chunk` objects, the route writes them as SSE frames and ends with `data: [DONE]`. A failure after the headers went out becomes an error frame, not a 500. With `stream: false`, `aggregate_chat` folds the same chunks into one `chat.completion`.","locale":"en"},"11064":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-159","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"The other protocols share this path; only its two ends differ:","locale":"en"},"11065":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-160","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"Endpoint","locale":"en"},"11066":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-161","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"On the way in","locale":"en"},"11067":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-162","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"On the way out","locale":"en"},"11068":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-163","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"`/v1/chat/completions`","locale":"en"},"11069":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-164","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"As is","locale":"en"},"11070":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-165","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"`emit_chat_chunks` / `aggregate_chat`","locale":"en"},"11071":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-166","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"`/v1/responses`","locale":"en"},"11072":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-167","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"`responses_to_chat` turns it into a Chat request","locale":"en"},"11073":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-168","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"`emit_responses_events` / `aggregate_responses`","locale":"en"},"11074":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-169","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"`/v1/messages`","locale":"en"},"11075":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-170","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"Passed through whole when the provider has a native Messages endpoint (`Registry.native_messages`); otherwise `messages_to_chat`","locale":"en"},"11076":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-171","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"Passed through, or `emit_messages_events` / `aggregate_messages`","locale":"en"},"11077":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-172","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-public-protocol","content":"A streamed Messages response sends `event: ping` every 15 seconds while the upstream is thinking, and closes the upstream connection when the client goes away (`hoshimi/api/heartbeat.py`). The Playground's conversations use the same route and accounting path; only the origin the usage is filed under differs.","locale":"en"},"11078":{"id":"/en/docs/development-architecture-173","page_id":"/en/docs/development-architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-architecture#the-command-line","content":"`get_app()` in `hoshimi/cli/app.py` is the one place every command is registered; each command's implementation is in `hoshimi/cli/commands/`. Typer's private imports (`typer._click`) are confined to `hoshimi/cli/factory.py`, so a Typer release that moves them breaks there first, and the command-line tests catch it. Every command takes `--debug` to turn on debug logging. `python -m hoshimi` is the same as the `hoshimi` command.","locale":"en"},"11079":{"id":"/en/docs/development-providers","page_id":"/en/docs/development-providers","type":"page","content":"Provider adapters","breadcrumbs":["Hoshimi Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-providers","locale":"en"},"11080":{"id":"/en/docs/development-providers-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-providers","content":"How to add a provider: the Provider contract, the normalized chunk protocol, error classification and account rotation, catalogues and capabilities, and interactive login flows.","locale":"en"},"11081":{"id":"/en/docs/development-providers-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-providers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-providers#kinds-of-provider","content":"Kinds of provider","locale":"en"},"11082":{"id":"/en/docs/development-providers-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-providers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-providers#the-provider-contract","content":"The Provider contract","locale":"en"},"11083":{"id":"/en/docs/development-providers-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-providers","type":"head